<% content_for :title, "Basecamp integration" %>

<div class="stack container--wide">
  <div>
    <h1>Basecamp integration</h1>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Turns a new post on a Basecamp message board into a draft announcement here — nothing
      publishes or gets sent to a platform automatically, someone still reviews and hits Publish
      from the announcement's page, same as any other announcement.
    </p>
  </div>

  <% if @token.blank? %>
    <div class="card card--outlined" style="border-color: var(--color-error);">
      <p class="field__error">
        <code class="font-mono">BASECAMP_WEBHOOK_TOKEN</code> isn't set, so this integration can't
        work yet. Generate one (any long random string — <code class="font-mono">SecureRandom.hex(32)</code>
        works) and set it in the environment Atlas runs in, then reload this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <section class="docs-section stack">
      <h2>Webhook URL</h2>
      <p>Paste this into a Basecamp project's webhook settings — see below for exactly where.</p>
      <pre class="code-block" style="word-break: break-all; white-space: pre-wrap;"><%= @webhook_url %></pre>
      <p class="field__hint">
        The token in this URL is what stands in for verification — Basecamp doesn't sign its
        webhook payloads the way Atlas's own outbound webhooks do (see the
        <%= link_to "webhook integration guide", docs_admin_webhooks_path %>). Keep this URL as
        private as a password; anyone with it can create draft announcements here. To rotate it,
        change <code class="font-mono">BASECAMP_WEBHOOK_TOKEN</code> and update every Basecamp
        project using the old URL.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section stack">
      <h2>Setup, in Basecamp</h2>
      <ol style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Open the Basecamp project you want posts from, then <strong>⚙️ → Webhooks</strong> (project settings).</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Add a new webhook</strong>.</li>
        <li>Paste the URL above into <strong>Payload URL</strong>.</li>
        <li>Under <strong>Active for</strong>, choose <strong>Only these events</strong> and check <strong>Message</strong> — no other event type is used, so leave the rest unchecked.</li>
        <li>Save. Basecamp sends a test ping right away; a real one arrives whenever someone posts a new message on that project's message board.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="field__hint">Repeat for every Basecamp project that should feed announcements into Atlas — the same URL works for all of them.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="docs-section stack">
      <h2>What ends up in the draft</h2>
      <ul class="stack" style="gap: 8px;">
        <li><strong>Title:</strong> the Basecamp post's subject line.</li>
        <li><strong>Content:</strong> the post's body, with a line appended noting which Basecamp project and person it came from and a link back to the original post.</li>
        <li><strong>Dates:</strong> starts today, ends one month out — Basecamp posts don't carry a date range, so these are just the same defaults as creating an announcement by hand; edit them before publishing if they don't fit.</li>
        <li><strong>Author:</strong> left blank — there's no Atlas account to attribute it to, so the Basecamp poster's name only appears in the content line above, not as this announcement's author.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="field__hint">
        Only a <strong>new</strong> message being posted creates a draft. Edits, comments, and
        everything else Basecamp can notify about are ignored — editing the post afterwards in
        Basecamp doesn't update the Atlas draft, since by then a person may have already changed
        it here too.
      </p>
    </section>
  <% end %>
</div>
